
Session: Responsible AI – Ethics, Risk and Responsibility
Date: October 24, 2026

In this session, I understood that building an AI product is not just about making it accurate or useful. 
We must also ensure that it is fair, safe, and trustworthy.

Professor explained how AI bias can arise from incomplete or unbalanced training data. The example of a child associating security guards with a 
particular community helped me understand that bias can develop from limited exposure, even without intentional discrimination.

Similarly, AI can produce unfair outcomes because of historical data, algorithms, or existing company policies.

I also learnt that data privacy requires us to collect only what is necessary, obtain appropriate consent, and clearly define how long data will be retained. 
In generative AI, hallucinations are another major risk. A product should know when to answer, when to abstain, and when to escalate a decision to a human.

The discussion on governance helped me understand that product managers must balance innovation with responsibility. 
Risk assessment should consider the likelihood and severity of an issue, whether it can be detected, and whether its effects can be reversed. 
Depending on the risk, we may need a limited rollout, additional human review, or a delay in launch.

I can relate these concepts to my work in HR automation and RPA. Automating a process is not enough if it produces incorrect or unfair outcomes.
AI solutions in HR must protect employee and candidate data, provide appropriate human oversight, and have clear escalation mechanisms.

My biggest takeaway is that responsible AI must be considered throughout the product lifecycle, not just before launch. 
As product managers, we are accountable for balancing business value with fairness, privacy, safety, and customer trust.
